  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \nSkies will be mostly cloudy today with a mix of mid and high \nlevel clouds. Isaias made landfall last evening as a Hurricane \nalong the Florida Coast near Destin and has since continued to \ntrack north while transitioning to a Post-Tropical Cyclone. Rain\nassociated with Isaias will spread north across the local area \nbetween 11 PM and 4 AM and will be moderate to heavy at times \nthrough Sunday afternoon. Rainfall amounts are still forecast to\nrange from a half inch to an inch and a half although some \nlocally higher amounts of 2 inches or more are possible, mainly\nacross Northeast Ohio. Streamflow values are currently low and \nwe expect to be able to handle most of the rain without many \nflooding concerns. People should clear drains of leaves and \ndebris to reduce flooding potential. The nearly vertically \nstacked system will move from Central Tennessee around 06Z \ntonight to West Virginia by 18Z Sunday. Mid level dry air does \nwrap north of the low during the late morning and early \nafternoon giving way to some breaks in precipitation. Depending \non the exact track of the system, showers are likely to fill \nback in, especially across eastern portions of the area with a \nfew thunderstorms possible. Will need to monitor for possible \nhigher rain rates with thunderstorms that could cause localized\nflooding problems. The bulk of the rain will exit the area to \nthe east by 8 PM Sunday.\n\nIn addition to the rain, southeast winds will become breezy Saturday \nevening as the surface pressure gradient tightens. Strong high \npressure will be over New England and the remnants of Isaias\nwill be approaching from the Tennessee Valley. A low level jet \nof 35-45 knots will move north tonight and could result in \nstronger wind gusts along the Erie PA shoreline. Winds will be \nsoutheasterly which is favorable for enhanced downsloping and \ngusts of 30-40 mph are possible, mainly before the steady rain \nbegins. Have increased winds in the forecast for late tonight \nearly Sunday morning but are forecast to remain below Advisory \ncriteria. Elsewhere wind gusts should tend to peak around 30 \nmph. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nThe remnants of Isaias will be off the New England Coast by \nMonday morning. Ridging builds overhead, both at the surface and\naloft, and breezy southwesterly winds will return on Monday \nafternoon. Monday will be another dry and warm day with highs in\nthe 70-80 degree range. Low pressure slides east into the Great\nLakes Region on Tuesday ahead of a trough that starts to break \ndown the upper ridge. Dewpoints near 60 degrees will allow for \nsome instability to develop ahead of the front with potential \nfor scattered showers and thunderstorms as a cold front pushes \nsouth Tuesday afternoon and evening. Highest potential for \nshowers and thunderstorms will be focused generally east of \nI-71. High temperatures on Wednesday will be nearly 10 degrees \ncooler than Tuesday. A stronger cold front is expected on \nFriday, ushering in the coolest air of the season to start next \nweekend.",
